package com.arise.app.game

import java.time.LocalDate
import java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit

/** A daily "wait, really?" card: a question first, the answer on tap. */
data class Card(val field: String, val question: String, val answer: String, val story: String)

object Knowledge {
    /** A fixed shuffle, so neighbouring days don't share a topic. Same card all day. */
    private val order: List<Int> by lazy { cards.indices.shuffled(java.util.Random(2026)) }

    fun forDay(origin: LocalDate, date: LocalDate): Card {
        val i = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(origin, date).toInt()
        return cards[order[Math.floorMod(i, cards.size)]]
    }

    val cards = listOf(
        Card("History", "Which is older: Oxford University or the Aztec Empire?", "Oxford, by about 200 years.",
            "Teaching was happening at Oxford by 1096. Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital, was founded in 1325. Old Europe and the 'ancient' Americas overlapped far more than we picture."),
        Card("History", "Did Cleopatra live closer in time to the building of the Great Pyramid, or to the Moon landing?", "The Moon landing.",
            "The Great Pyramid was finished around 2560 BCE. Cleopatra died in 30 BCE, about 2,500 years later. The Moon landing was 1969, about 2,000 years after her. To her, the pyramids were already ancient tourist sites."),
        Card("Biology", "How many hearts does an octopus have?", "Three. And its blood is blue.",
            "Two hearts pump blood through the gills, one pumps it to the body. Their blood uses copper (hemocyanin) instead of iron to carry oxygen, which turns it blue. The main heart actually stops while it swims, which is why octopuses prefer crawling."),
        Card("Plants", "Is a banana a berry? Is a strawberry?", "Banana: yes. Strawberry: no.",
            "To a botanist, a berry comes from one flower with one ovary and has seeds inside. Bananas, tomatoes and even cucumbers qualify. A strawberry's 'seeds' sit on the outside, so it's not a true berry at all."),
        Card("Space", "On Venus, which is longer: a day or a year?", "A day.",
            "Venus takes 243 Earth days to spin once, but only 225 to orbit the Sun. It also spins backwards, so there the Sun rises in the west."),
        Card("Earth", "Which came first on Earth: sharks or trees?", "Sharks, by tens of millions of years.",
            "Sharks show up in the fossil record around 450 million years ago. The first true trees appeared around 385 million years ago. Sharks have survived all five mass extinctions."),
        Card("Physics", "Is the Eiffel Tower the same height in summer and winter?", "No. It grows up to about 15 cm in summer.",
            "Iron expands when it heats up. On a hot day the whole tower stretches, and the sunny side expands more than the shade, so the top even leans slightly away from the Sun."),
        Card("Maths", "Which is bigger: the number of possible chess games, or the number of atoms in the observable universe?", "Chess games, by a mile.",
            "Claude Shannon estimated about 10^120 possible games. The observable universe has roughly 10^80 atoms. Chess came from India, as chaturanga, around the 6th century."),
        Card("Biology", "Which animal has survived being exposed to outer space?", "The tardigrade, or 'water bear'.",
            "In 2007, tardigrades rode outside a satellite in open space. Many survived the vacuum and radiation. They're half a millimetre long and live in moss, maybe on a wall near you."),
        Card("Body", "How often does your stomach replace its inner lining?", "Every few days.",
            "Your stomach acid is strong enough to dissolve metal, so the cells lining it are constantly replaced, every 3 to 5 days, along with a protective layer of mucus."),
        Card("Korean", "Why does Korea have its own alphabet instead of using Chinese characters?", "A king invented it so ordinary people could read.",
            "In 1443, King Sejong the Great created Hangul because Chinese characters took years to learn and only the elite could. He published it in 1446. Korea celebrates Hangul Day every 9 October."),
        Card("Korean", "What is 'nunchi' (눈치)?", "The Korean skill of reading the room.",
            "Nunchi literally means 'eye-measure': sensing what people think and feel without being told. Someone with fast nunchi knows when to talk, when to leave, and when someone needs help. You'll hear it in every K-drama."),
        Card("Korean", "Until 2023, how old was a Korean baby on the day it was born?", "One year old.",
            "In 'Korean age', you were 1 at birth and everyone got a year older on New Year's Day. A baby born on 31 December was 2 the next day. In June 2023, South Korea officially switched to international age."),
        Card("Animals", "What shape is wombat poop?", "Cubes.",
            "Wombats produce cube-shaped droppings, about 80 a night. A 2018 study found their intestines have stiff and stretchy sections that shape them. They stack them on rocks to mark territory."),
        Card("Space", "If you could drive a car to the Sun at 100 km/h, how long would it take?", "About 170 years.",
            "The Sun is about 150 million km away. Light covers that in 8 minutes 20 seconds. Your car would need more than two lifetimes, without bathroom breaks."),
        Card("Body", "Are you taller in the morning or at night?", "In the morning, by about 1 cm.",
            "During the day, gravity squeezes water out of the cushions (discs) between your vertebrae. At night, lying down, they soak it back up."),
        Card("History", "Were woolly mammoths still alive when the Great Pyramid was built?", "Yes.",
            "A small group of mammoths survived on Wrangel Island, off Siberia, until about 4,000 years ago. The Great Pyramid was built around 4,500 years ago."),
        Card("Tech", "Where does the term 'computer bug' come from?", "A real moth stuck in a computer in 1947.",
            "Engineers working on the Harvard Mark II found a moth trapped in a relay. They taped it into the logbook with the note 'First actual case of bug being found.' Grace Hopper loved telling the story."),
        Card("Biology", "Are most of the cells in your body human?", "Only about half.",
            "A 2016 estimate: around 30 trillion human cells and 38 trillion bacteria, mostly in your gut. By count you're roughly half microbe. By weight, the bacteria are only about 200 grams."),
        Card("Physics", "Can hot water freeze faster than cold water?", "Sometimes, yes. It's called the Mpemba effect.",
            "Erasto Mpemba, a Tanzanian student, noticed his hot ice-cream mix froze faster than cold mix in 1963. Scientists still argue about exactly why and when it happens."),
        Card("Language", "Which language has the most native speakers? And the most speakers in total?", "Mandarin for native speakers. English in total.",
            "About 940 million people grow up speaking Mandarin. English has around 1.5 billion speakers in total, but most learned it as a second language. Hindi is third in total."),
        Card("Space", "How many Earths would fit inside the Sun?", "About 1.3 million.",
            "And the Sun is an ordinary, medium-sized star. Some stars, like UY Scuti, could swallow billions of Suns."),
        Card("Body", "How many times will your heart beat in your lifetime?", "About 2.5 to 3 billion times.",
            "Around 100,000 beats a day, without a single day off. Regular cardio, like your runs, lowers your resting heart rate, so each beat does more work."),
        Card("India", "Where does the English word 'shampoo' come from?", "Hindi: 'chāmpo', to press or massage.",
            "Sake Dean Mahomed, from Patna, opened a 'shampooing' bath in Brighton, England in 1814, offering Indian head massage. It became so famous he was appointed shampooing surgeon to two British kings."),
        Card("India", "Which board game was invented in ancient India to teach about karma?", "Snakes and ladders.",
            "It was called Moksha Patam. Ladders were virtues like generosity, snakes were vices like anger. The British took it home and dropped the moral lessons."),
        Card("Maths", "If you could fold a sheet of paper 42 times, how thick would it be?", "Thick enough to reach the Moon.",
            "Each fold doubles the thickness. 0.1 mm doubled 42 times is about 440,000 km, more than the 384,000 km to the Moon. That's exponential growth, and it's how compound interest works too."),
        Card("Mind", "Why does time seem to go faster as you get older?", "Because fewer days feel new.",
            "Your brain records new experiences in detail and compresses routine ones. A week of firsts feels long when you look back; a month of identical days feels like nothing. New challenges literally make your year feel longer."),
        Card("Body", "Why do you get goosebumps?", "It's a leftover from when our ancestors had fur.",
            "Tiny muscles pull each hair upright. For furry animals, that traps warm air or makes them look bigger to a threat. We kept the reflex but lost the fur."),
        Card("Space", "What does space smell like?", "Like seared steak, hot metal and welding fumes.",
            "Astronauts coming back in from spacewalks report the smell on their suits. It's probably from molecules created by dying stars, called polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons."),
        Card("History", "How long did the Hundred Years' War last?", "116 years.",
            "England and France fought on and off from 1337 to 1453. Nobody fought for all 116 years: there were long truces, plague breaks and five different English kings."),
        Card("Tech", "How much memory did the computer that landed Apollo 11 on the Moon have?", "About 4 kilobytes of working memory.",
            "Your phone has millions of times more. The astronauts typed commands as two-number codes ('verb' and 'noun'). The software was led by Margaret Hamilton."),
        Card("Earth", "Why is the sea salty, but rivers aren't?", "Rivers carry salt in; only water leaves.",
            "Rain slowly dissolves minerals from rocks, and rivers carry them to the sea. The Sun evaporates pure water, leaving the salt behind. Over millions of years it built up."),
        Card("Body", "Which part of your body uses up most of the sugar from a meal?", "Your muscles.",
            "After a meal, skeletal muscle takes up the majority of the glucose in your blood. More muscle means a bigger 'sugar sink', which is why strength training helps so much with insulin resistance and PCOS."),
        Card("Body", "Does a short walk after eating actually do anything?", "Yes. Even 2 to 5 minutes lowers the sugar spike.",
            "A 2022 review found that light walking soon after a meal flattened blood-sugar spikes better than standing. Ten minutes is better. That's why your walk quests are timed after lunch and dinner."),
        Card("Mind", "How long does it really take to build a habit?", "66 days on average, not 21.",
            "In a 2009 UCL study, it took people between 18 and 254 days for a new habit to become automatic, with 66 days as the average. Missing a single day didn't break the process. Missing many did."),
        Card("Earth", "Which country has the most time zones?", "France, with 12.",
            "Not because of France itself, but because of overseas territories scattered from the Caribbean to the Pacific. Russia has 11, the USA 11 including territories."),
        Card("Space", "Where in our solar system might it rain diamonds?", "Inside Neptune and Uranus.",
            "Deep inside these ice giants, crushing pressure may squeeze carbon into diamonds that sink like hail. Lab experiments in 2017 recreated the conditions and made nano-diamonds."),
        Card("History", "Did Vikings wear horned helmets?", "No. That came from an 1876 opera.",
            "No horned Viking helmet has ever been found. The look comes from costumes designed for Wagner's opera cycle 'The Ring of the Nibelung'. Real Viking helmets were simple and round."),
        Card("Money", "What was 'tulip mania'?", "A 1630s Dutch bubble where single tulip bulbs sold for fortunes.",
            "Rare striped tulips became status symbols, and people traded contracts for bulbs that were still in the ground. In February 1637 buyers stopped showing up and prices crashed. It's often called the first speculative bubble."),
        Card("Science", "Is old window glass thicker at the bottom because glass flows?", "No. Glass is a solid; old glass was just made unevenly.",
            "Old glass panes vary in thickness from how they were made, and builders usually put the thick side down. Glass is an amorphous solid: it would take far longer than the age of the universe to sag visibly."),
        Card("Animals", "Why are flamingos pink?", "Because of what they eat.",
            "Flamingos are born grey. Pigments called carotenoids in the algae and brine shrimp they eat turn their feathers pink. In zoos, keepers add these pigments to their food."),
        Card("Space", "Are there more stars in the universe or grains of sand on all of Earth's beaches?", "Stars, by a lot.",
            "Rough estimates put Earth's beach sand at about 10^19 grains and the observable universe's stars at 10^22 to 10^24. Every grain of sand on every beach, times a thousand or more."),
        Card("Body", "What's the hardest substance in your body?", "Tooth enamel.",
            "It's harder than bone and made mostly of a mineral called hydroxyapatite. But it can't regrow, which is why sugary drinks sipped all day are so harsh on teeth."),
        Card("Mind", "Why can't you tickle yourself?", "Your brain predicts your own touch and cancels it.",
            "Part of your brain called the cerebellum predicts the feeling of your own movements and turns down the response. A surprise touch from someone else can't be predicted, so it tickles."),
        Card("Earth", "Is Mount Everest the tallest mountain on Earth?", "Highest above sea level, yes. Tallest from base to peak, no.",
            "Mauna Kea in Hawaii starts deep on the ocean floor. Measured from its base, it's about 10,200 m tall, well over Everest's 8,849 m."),
        Card("Physics", "How can you tell how far away a thunderstorm is?", "Count the seconds between the flash and the thunder, then divide by 3.",
            "Light reaches you almost instantly, but sound travels about 1 km every 3 seconds. Nine seconds means the lightning was about 3 km away."),
        Card("Plants", "Can plants warn each other about danger?", "Yes, in their own chemical way.",
            "When caterpillars chew on some plants, they release scents into the air. Neighbouring plants pick them up and start producing defence chemicals before they're attacked themselves."),
        Card("India", "Which Indian city is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world?", "Varanasi.",
            "People have lived there for at least 3,000 years. Mark Twain wrote that it was 'older than history, older than tradition, older even than legend'."),
        Card("Animals", "Do cows have best friends?", "It looks like it.",
            "A 2013 study found cows' heart rates and stress levels dropped when they were penned with their preferred partner, compared with a random cow."),
        Card("Animals", "Which animal sleeps up to 22 hours a day?", "The koala.",
            "Koalas eat eucalyptus leaves, which are low in energy and hard to digest. Sleeping most of the day is how they save energy."),
        Card("Body", "Why does your body need sleep to burn fat well?", "Short sleep raises hunger hormones and lowers insulin sensitivity.",
            "After just a few nights of short sleep, studies find more ghrelin (the hunger hormone), less leptin (the 'full' signal) and worse blood sugar control. Your lights-off quest is a fat-loss quest."),
        Card("Tech", "What does the 'www' in web addresses stand for, and who invented it?", "World Wide Web, invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989.",
            "He was working at CERN, the physics lab in Switzerland, and wanted scientists to share documents easily. He never patented it, so it's free for everyone."),
        Card("Mind", "Why do you remember unfinished tasks better than finished ones?", "The Zeigarnik effect.",
            "In the 1920s, Bluma Zeigarnik noticed waiters remembered unpaid orders perfectly and forgot them once paid. An unfinished task keeps a 'tab' open in your mind. Starting something, even for 2 minutes, makes it easier to come back to."),
        Card("Biology", "Can you sneeze with your eyes open?", "Yes, it's possible.",
            "Closing your eyes is a reflex, not protection. The myth that your eyes would pop out is false. Most people just can't override the reflex."),
        Card("Money", "Why does starting early matter so much with saving?", "Because of compounding.",
            "Saving ₹2,000 a month at 12% a year: starting at 21 gives about 2.1 crore by 60. Starting at 31 gives about 62 lakh. Ten years earlier, more than 3 times the money."),
    )
}
